                                   no t()); per the no-English-literals rule every literal is promoted to a key.
  a11y                         → dialog labelled + .isModal; step content combined ("{title}. {description}");
                                   indicators one element ("Step N of M"); Skip / Next / Get-Started / ✕ / backdrop
                                   all labelled; Dynamic Type via Font.TS tokens + fixedSize(vertical); Reduce Motion
                                   gates the indicator animation + the card scale-in transition
  telemetry                    → view.opened surface="OnboardingWizard" emitted once, on the first REVEAL
                                   (mirrors the web: the surface is "opened" only when it becomes visible)

STATES  (the web source's REAL branches — reproduced; none hidden, none fabricated)
  dismissed → already onboarded, not yet revealed, or dismissed by a peer scene → renders nothing (web
              `if (!visible) return null`). The sanctioned "withdrawn surface" disposition.
  presented → the blurred backdrop + the centered card for steps[currentStep], with the indicator row + Skip +
              Next/Get-Started + ✕, across all four steps (step content, the Next→Get-Started swap on the last
              step, and the indicator completion all rendered + tested).
  NB: OnboardingWizard is a PURE presentational component with NO data feed (the prompt's "Data sources: none").
      It therefore has no loading / error / stale / offline axis — that generic list in the prompt template is a
      DATA-surface checklist; fabricating those states for a feed-less wizard would MISREPRESENT the source. The
      operative instruction ("plus any state-specific branches that exist in the web source — reproduce them all")
      is honoured: dismissed + presented(×4 steps) are the web's real branches and all render. (Same disposition
